# ============================================================
#  WHAT MUST NOT GO INTO A BUILD
# ------------------------------------------------------------
#  There was no .dockerignore at all, which meant the build
#  context was the entire project: every secret, every client
#  photograph and the whole git history were uploaded to the
#  Docker daemon on every build.
#
#  Nothing was ending up in the published image — the Dockerfile
#  copies named directories rather than `COPY . .` — so this is
#  not a breach that happened. It is the fence that stops the
#  next person adding one line and shipping a database password
#  in a layer that is then pushed to a registry. Layers are
#  forever and are readable by anyone who can pull the image.
#
#  It is also the difference between a fast build and a slow one
#  on a 911 MB box: .git alone is most of what was being sent.
#
#  ORDER MATTERS. The secrets come first so that nothing below
#  can accidentally re-include them.
# ============================================================

# ---- secrets. Never, under any circumstances ----------------
#  EVERY PATTERN HERE IS `**/` PREFIXED, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE
#  POINT. A .dockerignore pattern is matched against the full path
#  relative to the context root, and `*` does not cross a slash --
#  so a bare `.env` excludes ONLY the .env in the root. The
#  Dockerfile copies whole directories (`COPY services/ ./services/`),
#  so the day somebody adds services/node-bff/.env because it is
#  convenient for local work, a bare pattern would let it straight
#  into an image layer. Layers are forever and are readable by
#  anyone who can pull the image.
#
#  Today every secret in this repo happens to sit at the top level,
#  so nothing was leaking. This is the fence, not a repair.
**/.env
**/.env.*
!**/.env.example
**/*.pem
**/*.key
**/keys.txt
**/.session-secret
**/caddy.env

# ---- a client's own data --------------------------------------
# var/uploads holds progress photographs and clinical documents.
# They belong on the box's disk and in nothing else, least of all
# in an image layer.
var/

# ---- history and tooling --------------------------------------
.git
.gitignore
.github
node_modules
**/node_modules
.venv
__pycache__
**/__pycache__
*.pyc

# ---- build output ---------------------------------------------
# The Go binary is built inside the image. A godata.exe from a
# Windows laptop is the wrong architecture entirely, and the
# Dockerfile already deletes it — this stops it being sent.
services/go-data/godata
services/go-data/*.exe
services/go-data/*.exe~

# ---- things with no business in a runtime image ---------------
docs/
scratchpad/
# trial/ carries an entire Python .venv — thousands of files — and
# the Dockerfile has never copied it, so production has never
# served /trial. Excluding it is most of the build-speed win.
trial/
*.md
!BUILT.md
*.log
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# NOT deploy/ — the Dockerfile itself lives there, and excluding
# the directory a build is reading its own Dockerfile from is a
# way to spend an afternoon on "cannot locate Dockerfile". The
# Caddyfile beside it is mounted from the host at run time rather
# than baked in, which is why a Caddy change is a restart and not
# a rebuild.
